The advent of a renewable, edible and porous framework1 — namely, CD-MOFs derived from $\gamma\cdot$ -cyclodextrin2 (γ-CD) and alkali metal salts—constitutes a class of porous frameworks that can be synthesized from completely nontoxic, naturally occurring starting materials on a large scale.3 Since the serendipitous discovery3a of CD-MOFs in 2010 and as a result of their ease of synthesis and the ready availability of their building blocks, they have found applications in gas sorption,3b,4 sensors,5 drug delivery,6 encapsulation of volatile compounds and fullerenes7 in addition to hydrocarbon separations.3e,8
One of the unique features of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) is the flexibility in their design and syntheses.9 Postsynthetic modifications of MOFs, leading to the introduction of other metals into their secondary building units (SBUs) have been achieved,10 while retaining their porous frameworks and the structural integrities. Furthermore, the organic linkers/struts in MOFs can also be exchanged11 through the use of ingenious postsynthetic techniques to install targeted functionalities. The incorporation of different metal ions into the frameworks of MOFs, in order to form mixed-metal organic frameworks, has become relevant in recent years in relation to attempts to explore applications in various fields, e.g., heterogeneous catalysis,11b charge carriers,12 gas uptake,10b,13 and photocatalytic activity.10d
Although several alkali metal salts have been employed in the generation of CD-MOFs, there are only three porous frameworks3b,3c— CD-MOF-1, CD-MOF-2, and CD-MOF-3 — obtained from γ-CD and the alkali metal cations $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{{Rb}^{+}}$ , and ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ respectively. The challenge in preparing CD-MOFs with cations, other than these three alkali metal ions, lies in an inability to obtain crystalline highly porous frameworks.3d,14 It transpires that the various alkali metals salts — e.g., KOH, RbOH, CsOH, $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{CO}}_{3}$ , KF, KCl, KBr, $\mathrm{NaBPh}_{4}-\mathrm{form}^{3\mathrm{c}}$ cubic crystals with $\gamma$ -CD, occupying the space group $I432$ . Among them all, only CD-MOFs-1–3, synthesized from KOH, RbOH and $\mathrm{CsOH}.$ , respectively, have been characterized3 by full crystal structural analyses. Herein, we demonstrate a synthetic methodology which overcomes the limitation of preparing porous CD-MOFs with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions by co-crystallizing KOH and LiOH with $\gamma$ -CD following all our attempts to crystallize $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ with LiOH alone to afford the prototypical porous structure of CD-MOF were not successful. Supporting Information, Figure S1. In the light of this failure, we turned our attention to the development of a methodology that relies on the co-crystallization of LiOH and KOH with $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ to produce partially $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ion-substituted CD-MOF — hereafter referred to as Li/K-CD-MOF — by replacing some of the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites in CD-MOF-1 with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions.
The synthetic protocol for CD-MOF-1, i.e., CD-MOF-1 is synthesized by MeOH vapor diffusion into an aqueous solution of 8.0 equiv of KOH and 1.0 equiv of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ during 7 days to afford a crop of colorless, cubic, single crystals in about $60\%$ yield was adopted. $^{3\mathrm{a-c}}\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{K-CD-MOF}$ was prepared by combining 8.0 equiv of KOH and LiOH — molar ratios of LiOH / KOH varied from $\mathrm{Li}_{0.2:}\mathrm{K}_{1.4}$ to $\mathrm{Li}_{1.4}{:}\mathrm{K}_{0.2}$ — and 1.0 equiv of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ in aqueous solution. See Supporting Information Table S1. Vapor diffusion of MeOH into these solutions during 15 days resulted in cubic, colorless single crystals, suitable for X-ray crystallography, in 43- $50\%$ yield. The proportions of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions occupying $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites in Li/K-CD-MOFs were confirmed by Inductively Coupled Plasma Optical Emission Spectroscopy (ICP-OES). The Li/K-CD-MOF crystals were washed well with MeOH in order to remove any unreacted LiOH from the crystals before ICP-OES analysis. Li/KCD-MOFs were digested in $5\%$ aqueous ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ solution. ICPOES analyses confirmed that the proportions of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions in the mixed-metal CD-MOF increased on upping the amounts of LiOH employed in the co-crystallization of Li/K-CD-MOFs. See Supporting Information Table S2. The highest $\mathrm{Li^{+}/K^{+}}$ ratio — namely, $\mathrm{K}_{1.18}\mathrm{Li}_{0.61}$ — we were able to obtain in Li/K-CD-MOFs was achieved from the co-crystallization of LiOH and KOH in a ratio of 1.4:0.2 with respect to 1 mol equiv $\gamma$ -CD.
Single crystal X-ray analysis was performed on the sample of Li/K-CD-MOF containing the highest proportion of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions as indicated by ICP-OES (Table S2). In contrast to CD-MOF-1, which is composed3a of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ tori held together by $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions in a cubic unit cell of space group $I432$ , the solid-state structure (Figure 1) of this Li/K-CD-MOF occupies the trigonal space group, R32 $(\gamma=120^{\circ})$ . See Supporting Information Table S3.
In comparison with CD-MOF-1,3a two adjacent binding sites for $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions on $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ tori are partially occupied (Figure 1a,b) by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions with occupancies of 0.47 and 0.53, respectively. The $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions are coordinated to six of the eight $\boldsymbol{a}$ -Dglucopyranosyl residues in an alternating $1^{\circ}/2^{\circ}$ face fashion. Although the unit cell of Li/K-CD-MOF is different from that of the CD-MOF-1, we were still able to observe (Figure 1c) the slightly twisted $(\gamma\mathrm{-CD})_{6}$ repeating cubes with spherical cavities of diameter ${\sim}1.7~\mathrm{nm}$ which are assembled from six $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ tori through coordination with both $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions to the C-6 OH groups and the glycosidic ring O atoms on the inwardly directed primary $(1^{\circ})$ faces of alternating $\alpha{-}D$ -glucopyranosyl residues around the $\gamma$ -CD tori. The ratio of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions to $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was found to be $2(\mathrm{K}_{1.23}\mathrm{Li}_{0.77}){:}1$ which is very similar to the results obtained from ICP-OES analysis, $\mathrm{K}_{1.18}\mathrm{Li}_{0.61}$ . The absence of coordination of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions on two neighboring $\alpha-D$ -glucopyranosyl residues of each $\gamma$ -CD tori results in the distortion of a regular $(\gamma\mathrm{-CD})_{6}$ cube. The distorted $(\gamma\mathrm{-CD})_{6}$ cubes pack in an infinite pseudo-bodycentered cubic manner (Figure 1d) through the coordination of $\dot{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions to the C-2 and $\mathrm{C}{-}3~\mathrm{OH}$ groups of the second set of alternating $\alpha{-}D$ -glucopyranosyl residues on the outwardly oriented secondary $(2^{\circ})$ faces of the $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ tori.
$\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites substitution by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions were located based on the absence of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions in the framework. Since $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions are the heaviest atoms in the structure, the electron density and proportion of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions can be determined with accuracy. The site occupancy of the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions in the crystal structure was refined and the missing $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions were substituted with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions. This type of substitution is possible because (i) the chemistry and coordination sphere of the two alkali metal ions is similar.15 Therefore, it is expected that the $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions will share the same site as the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions, (ii) each of the alkali metal ion sites is fully occupied — that is, there cannot be vacancies at the alkali cation sites. Full occupancy is assumed on the account of the cation $\textit{\textperthousand}$ -CD ratio in CD-MOF-1, where the proportion of cations must balance the $\gamma$ -CD. Therefore, the occupancies of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions are complementary, and (iii) any disorder in the $\gamma$ -CD molecules does not affect the overall proportion of the alkali ions since the number of $\gamma$ -CD molecules does not change.
Powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) experiments performed on CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF confirm (Figure 2a) that their extended structures remain intact upon the substitution of some $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions. The XRD patterns for CD-MOF-1 and Li/KCD-MOF can also be compared with the simulated pattern obtained from the single crystal analysis. The comparison demonstrates that the crystallographic properties remain intact upon substitution of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites of CD-MOF-1 by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ion in Li/K-CDMOF. Furthermore, we have not noted any changes in the PXRD patterns of Li/K-CD-MOFs when the proportions of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions to $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions are varied. See Supporting Information Figure S2.
The permanent porosities of CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF were examined (Figure 2b) by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . Characteristic Type I isotherms, which are observed for CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF, confirm the presence of highly microporous materials. The Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface areas of CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF were calculated to be 1145 and $1205~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Although some of the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites in CD-MOF-1 are replaced by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions during cocrystallization, we detected no more than a $5\%$ difference in the BET surface area. The pore size distribution, which was calculated using non-linear density functional theory (NLDFT), shows identical pore maxima centered on $11.7\mathrm{\AA}$ , confirming (Figure 2b, Inset) the preservation of the pore size distribution after occupation of the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions. Thermogravimetric analysis was also conducted (Figure 3a) in order to investigate the thermal stabilities of CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF under $\Nu_{2}$ . The initial mass loss up to $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in both samples can be attributed to the evolution of trapped MeOH from their porous extended frameworks. CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF were found to be thermally stable up to $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an inert atmosphere. In both samples, further mass loss can be attributed to the degradation of the $\gamma$ -CD units which comes into play above $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The use of the accessible porosity of Li/K-CD-MOF for gas sorption analyses has also been investigated by studying the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas adsorption isotherms and comparing them with those for CD-MOF-1. These experiments exhibit the integrity of the structural porosity associated with the partially $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ion-substituted Li/K-CD-MOF. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms for
CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF were carried out at 273 (Figure 2b) and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . See Supporting Information Figure S3. The addition of nucleophilic reagents — e.g., primary amines, in porous materials improves16 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture by forming carbamates or bicarbonates. Likewise, the primary $(1^{\circ})$ hydroxyl groups are also known to be more reactive than their secondary $(2^{\circ})$ counterparts and these contribute to the reversible carbonic acid formation.3b,17 CD-MOF-1 and Li/K-CD-MOF show the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of 4.2 and $4.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{K}$ , and 3.0 and $3.\bar{1}\ \mathrm{mmol\g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. A slight increase in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity may correspond to the lower density of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions compared with $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions. Indeed, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of Li/K-CD-MOF and CD-MOF-1 is higher than that of CD-MOF-2 — namely, 1.7 and $1.4~\mathrm{mmol~g}^{-1}$ , respectively, at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ .3a-c It is noteworthy that the most of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacity was observed at low partial pressures — below 0.15 bar — in the case of both samples.
The initial steep rise, which is observed at very low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressures, regardless of the temperature,16 is believed to be the result of a chemisorption process. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of Li/K-CDMOF were also carried out at $77~\mathrm{K}$ up to 1 bar. See Supporting Information Figure S4. Porous materials containing $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions are excellent when it comes to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage,18 owing to the low density of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions and open metal sites. Li/K-CD-MOF and CD-MOF-1 shows $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of 126 and $121{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, respectively, which surpasses the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity3c of $100~\mathrm{c}\mathrm{\dot{m}}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for CD-MOF-2. We believe that the higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity in Li/K-CD-MOF originates from the lower density of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions compared with $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions.
In summary, we have tackled the long-standing constraints of generating CD-MOFs, from other than $\bar{\mathsf{K}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{{Rb}^{+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ ions, by preparing extended frameworks containing $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions. The $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions incorporated into the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites of CD-MOF-1 can be accomplished by co-crystallization of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions with $\gamma$ -CD without sacrificing the porous architecture of the CD-MOFs. The partial substitution of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions in the frameworks has been established by determining the vacancies associated with the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites. Some of the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ sites are occupied by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions, which share the same coordination geometry as $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions. The $\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{K}$ -CDMOF with the highest $\mathrm{Li}^{+}/\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ratio is stable upon removal of solvent and has revealed surface areas of approximately $1205~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ which is almost identical to that of CD-MOF-1. Moreover, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture capacity is not hindered by the occupying $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions and show $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of 4.5 and $3.1\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. This findings show that our inability of synthesize CD-MOFs with alkali metal ions other than $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , $\dot{\mathrm{Rb}^{+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ can be addressed by employing a co-crystallization methodology. Li/K-CD-MOF could potentially be employed as a psychiatric medication to treat bipolar disorder.19
